---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Section Summary: Data</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-4" />

        <p class="text-secondary">
          Activities 2.14 &ndash; 2.20. Use this to review, or to fill in a definition you missed.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">1.</span> Binary Numbers and What Bits Represent</h2>

          <p>
            Place value works the
            same in base 2 as in base 10. The same bits mean different things
            depending on what you agree they represent.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Bit</dt>
            <dd>Short for binary digit. Either 0 or 1.</dd>
            <dt>Byte</dt>
            <dd>Eight bits.</dd>
            <dt>Place value</dt>
            <dd>The base raised to the power of the position, counting from 0 on the right.</dd>
            <dt>Abstraction</dt>
            <dd>Reducing complexity by focusing on the main idea and hiding what does not matter here.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">2.</span> p5 Lab 10: Color as Data</h2>

          <p>
            A color is three numbers. How many
            bits each one gets decides how many colors are possible at
            all.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Channel</dt>
            <dd>One of the three numbers &mdash; red, green, or blue &mdash; that make a color.</dd>
            <dt>Bit depth</dt>
            <dd>How many bits are used per channel, which sets how many levels are possible.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">3.</span> The Limits of Bits</h2>

          <p>
            A fixed number of bits can hold only so
            much and only so precisely. Sampling turns a smooth signal into
            values a computer can store.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Overflow error</dt>
            <dd>An error that occurs when a computer tries to handle a number outside the defined range of values.</dd>
            <dt>Round-off error</dt>
            <dd>The error introduced when a real number is stored as an approximation.</dd>
            <dt>Analog data</dt>
            <dd>Data whose values change smoothly rather than in discrete intervals.</dd>
            <dt>Sampling</dt>
            <dd>Measuring the value of an analog signal at regular intervals.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">4.</span> Compression</h2>

          <p>
            Compression sells redundancy. Whether you can
            get the original back exactly is the difference between the two
            kinds.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Data compression</dt>
            <dd>Reducing the number of bits stored or transmitted.</dd>
            <dt>Redundancy</dt>
            <dd>Repetition in the original data. It is what compression exploits.</dd>
            <dt>Lossless</dt>
            <dd>Guarantees complete reconstruction of the original.</dd>
            <dt>Lossy</dt>
            <dd>Allows reconstruction of an approximation only, and usually saves more.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">5.</span> Information, Metadata, and Correlation</h2>

          <p>
            Data becomes
            information once you extract facts from it. Two things moving
            together is not, on its own, one causing the
            other.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Information</dt>
            <dd>The collection of facts and patterns extracted from data.</dd>
            <dt>Metadata</dt>
            <dd>Data about data. Used for finding, organizing, and managing information.</dd>
            <dt>Correlation</dt>
            <dd>Two things moving together. Not by itself evidence that one causes the other.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">6.</span> Cleaning, Bias, and Scale</h2>

          <p>
            Cleaning makes data uniform
            without changing what it means. Bias comes from how the data was
            collected, so collecting more of it does not
            help.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Cleaning data</dt>
            <dd>Making data uniform without changing its meaning.</dd>
            <dt>Invalid data</dt>
            <dd>Entries that cannot be true, whatever the source says.</dd>
            <dt>Bias</dt>
            <dd>A problem created by the type or source of the data collected. Not fixed by collecting more.</dd>
            <dt>Scalability</dt>
            <dd>Whether a system's capacity can keep up as the data grows.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">7.</span> p5 Lab 11: Visualizing a Dataset</h2>

          <p>
            A chart is a program, and
            every choice in it is an argument. Where the axis starts can change
            the story without changing a single number.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Visualization</dt>
            <dd>A chart, graph, or other visual form used to communicate what was found in data.</dd>
            <dt>Baseline</dt>
            <dd>The value an axis starts counting from. Choosing it is a decision, not a fact.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  dl {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  dt {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  dd {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }
  section {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
</style>
